WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.750
Pick any star in the night sky.

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:07.050
We might know its name, what kind of star it is and how far away it is.

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:09.820
But we probably don’t know it’s age.

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:12.934
Stars don’t exactly come with a birth certificate.

00:00:12.934 --> 00:00:17.933
Even for our own sun, we know how big it is, how massive it is, what it's made of...

00:00:17.933 --> 00:00:21.020
But we can't tell how long it’s been there just by looking at it.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:25.638
Star ages might help us figure out how galaxies grow and evolve,

00:00:25.638 --> 00:00:27.752
how planets form,

00:00:27.752 --> 00:00:32.851
and maybe even assist in the search for life in other solar systems.

00:00:33.035 --> 00:00:36.730
So how do you measure the age of a star?

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:40.395
Scientists have to estimate a star’s age by looking at things

00:00:40.395 --> 00:00:42.122
like its spin and its brightness.

00:00:42.122 --> 00:00:45.907
And things depend on where a star is in its life cycle.

00:00:46.275 --> 00:00:49.473
The average star is born from a spinning cloud of gas

00:00:49.473 --> 00:00:51.969
that collapses in on itself.

00:00:51.969 --> 00:00:57.042
The gas at the center gets so hot and dense that it sparks nuclear fusion,

00:00:57.042 --> 00:00:59.493
turning hydrogen into helium.

00:00:59.493 --> 00:01:02.930
This fuel jump starts the star’s engine.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:06.278
The star spends most of its life with this engine running.

00:01:06.278 --> 00:01:09.316
As a star matures, its core condenses,

00:01:09.316 --> 00:01:13.770
and this hydrogen burning zone moves toward the surface.

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:18.680
When a star runs out of hydrogen fuel in its core, it enters retirement.

00:01:19.006 --> 00:01:22.943
The star cools down at the surface and becomes a red giant.

00:01:22.943 --> 00:01:26.280
Some stars continue fusing helium and other elements

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:29.507
into even heavier elements before they die.

00:01:29.507 --> 00:01:31.685
And in the finale of this lifecycle,

00:01:31.685 --> 00:01:35.649
smaller stars release the rest of their gas into space,

00:01:35.649 --> 00:01:38.215
while more massive ones can go supernova.

00:01:38.215 --> 00:01:42.459
A star’s lifespan and its ultimate fate are set by its mass.

00:01:42.459 --> 00:01:45.230
More massive stars burn through their fuel more quickly

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:47.575
and may only live a few million years.

00:01:47.575 --> 00:01:51.411
Less massive stars like our sun use their fuel more slowly

00:01:51.411 --> 00:01:54.063
and stick around for 10 billion years or more.

00:01:54.063 --> 00:01:56.542
That means two stars could be the same age

00:01:56.542 --> 00:01:59.119
but in totally different phases of their lives.

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:02.789
Every galaxy is littered with clusters of stars.

00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:06.496
And while stars in the same cluster burn fuel at different speeds,

00:02:06.496 --> 00:02:09.257
they were probably all born around the same time.

00:02:09.257 --> 00:02:12.774
That means scientists can ballpark the age of a group of stars

00:02:12.774 --> 00:02:15.659
by figuring out which ones have just run out of fuel.

00:02:15.659 --> 00:02:20.620
They do that by plotting the stars on a Hertzsprung-Russell or H-R diagram.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:25.140
Take the Pleiades Cluster in the constellation Taurus.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.685
Measure the brightness and temperature of each star,

00:02:27.685 --> 00:02:29.430
and plot them on this graph.

00:02:29.794 --> 00:02:35.000
The stars that are still burning hydrogen in their cores fall along this line here.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.931
Scientists call this the main sequence.

00:02:37.931 --> 00:02:40.621
The Pleiades are about 100 million years old.

00:02:40.621 --> 00:02:44.363
All but the most massive stars are still on the main sequence.

00:02:44.363 --> 00:02:46.718
When those stars reach their twilight years,

00:02:46.718 --> 00:02:49.767
they will veer off that main path and end up over here.

00:02:49.767 --> 00:02:52.855
The most massive stars leave the main sequence first.

00:02:52.855 --> 00:02:57.959
Figuring out which stars have most recently made that turn tells you that those stars

00:02:57.959 --> 00:03:01.862
have just used up all of the hydrogen fuel in their cores.

00:03:01.862 --> 00:03:03.783
Based on previous calculations,

00:03:03.783 --> 00:03:06.204
scientists know how long that should take,

00:03:06.204 --> 00:03:10.000
and thus how long ago the stars began to twinkle.

00:03:10.573 --> 00:03:15.515
If the star you’re curious about happens to be in a cluster, H-R diagrams are great.

00:03:15.515 --> 00:03:18.441
But it’s a lot harder to gauge the age of a single star.

00:03:18.543 --> 00:03:22.559
One way is to measure how fast the star spins.

00:03:22.559 --> 00:03:27.034
Surface activity, star spots and the spectrum of light the star emits

00:03:27.034 --> 00:03:30.475
can tell scientists a star’s rotation speed.

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:35.196
In the 1970s, astronomers noticed that stars in young clusters

00:03:35.196 --> 00:03:38.106
spin faster than stars in older clusters.

00:03:38.106 --> 00:03:40.335
That observation led to an equation

00:03:40.335 --> 00:03:44.232
that can convert spin into age, more or less.

00:03:44.232 --> 00:03:46.480
But rotation is hard to measure.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:51.113
Measuring the rotation rate of every star in the universe is impractical.

00:03:51.113 --> 00:03:54.123
And for some stars, it’s simply impossible.

00:03:54.123 --> 00:03:57.844
For now, the most accurate way to measure the age of a single star

00:03:57.844 --> 00:04:01.687
is by tracking how its brightness changes over time.

00:04:01.687 --> 00:04:07.220
Those periodic changes reflect pulses and shivers inside the star.

00:04:07.772 --> 00:04:12.250
This stellar seismology can tell us how dense the core is

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:15.747
and how much hydrogen the star has converted into helium.

00:04:15.747 --> 00:04:18.900
The more hydrogen in the core, the younger the star.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:23.066
To do this, you need to point a really good space telescope at a star

00:04:23.066 --> 00:04:24.717
for a long time.

00:04:24.717 --> 00:04:28.208
That’s what the Kepler space telescope did when was looking for exoplanets.

00:04:28.208 --> 00:04:32.449
To the exoplanet scientists, all the data that didn’t show a planet was trash.

00:04:32.449 --> 00:04:34.661
But to stellar physicists it was treasure.

00:04:34.661 --> 00:04:38.045
The Kepler observations showed something surprising:

00:04:38.045 --> 00:04:40.758
stars eventually stop slowing down.

00:04:40.758 --> 00:04:43.321
Once stars have burned about half of their fuel,

00:04:43.321 --> 00:04:47.890
they may keep the same rotation rates for the rest of their lives.

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:52.434
That makes spin an unreliable proxy for stars beyond middle age,

00:04:52.434 --> 00:04:56.170
and confirms that brightness provides more accurate numbers.

00:04:56.170 --> 00:05:00.105
New telescopes will follow in Kepler’s footsteps

00:05:00.105 --> 00:05:05.000
and could provide even better age estimates for the stars they observe.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:11.237
A star’s age is more than just a fun fact about stars we recognize in the night sky.

00:05:11.237 --> 00:05:14.208
Better age estimates for stars in the Milky Way

00:05:14.208 --> 00:05:18.746
could help us understand the evolution of the galaxy--and our own star.

00:05:18.803 --> 00:05:21.441
And of course, there’s aliens.

00:05:21.584 --> 00:05:25.635
When we find life on another planet, we’ll want to know how long it’s been there.

00:05:25.635 --> 00:05:28.868
Knowing the age of the star can tell you roughly the age of the planet,

00:05:28.868 --> 00:05:32.491
and that can give you an idea of how long it took for that life to get going.

00:05:32.902 --> 00:05:36.281
So when you ask, how old is that star?

00:05:36.281 --> 00:05:42.454
You’re asking one of the most fundamental questions in the universe.

00:05:46.121 --> 00:05:50.513
So when I made my first H-R diagram in college as part of a class,

00:05:50.513 --> 00:05:52.612
That was when I realized for the first time:

00:05:52.612 --> 00:05:55.122
That's the only way you can tell how old a star is.

00:05:55.122 --> 00:05:56.627
And that kind of blew my mind.

00:05:56.627 --> 00:05:57.792
I was like, that's all?

00:05:57.792 --> 00:05:59.198
That's the best we can do?

00:05:59.198 --> 00:06:02.557
Like this is such a basic thing and you can measure--

00:06:02.557 --> 00:06:05.237
I thought for sure you could tell from like the spectrum or something.

00:06:05.237 --> 00:06:06.544
But no, you can't.

00:06:07.558 --> 00:06:08.841
You're going to be famous.

00:06:09.252 --> 00:06:10.672
You're going to be on YouTube.

